# Consistent Return Shape

Components should prefer returning the **same root element type** regardless of internal state. State branching should happen *inside* the consistent structure via an internal render helper, rather than returning entirely different component trees at the top level.

## Avoid

```tsx
// ❌ Avoid — three different root elements; consumer cannot predict what they receive
function ItemList({ data, status, error }: Props) {
  if (status === 'pending') return <ListSkeleton />
  if (status === 'error') return <Alert severity="error">{error.message}</Alert>

  return <ul>{data.map((item) => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}</ul>
}
```

## Preferred Pattern

```tsx
// ✅ Preferred — always returns <Card>; only the inner content varies by state
function ItemList({ data, status }: Props) {
  const renderContent = () => {
    if (status === 'pending') return <LoadingSkeleton />
    if (status === 'error') return <ErrorMessage />
    if (!data || data.length === 0) return <EmptyState />

    return data.map((item) => <ItemRow key={item.id} item={item} />)
  }

  return (
    <Card>
      <CardHeader title="Items" />
      <CardContent>{renderContent()}</CardContent>
    </Card>
  )
}
```

The root `<Card>` is always returned. State handling is fully encapsulated inside `renderContent()`.

## Guidelines

1. Prefer a consistent root structure — vary internal content via a render helper, not the root element
2. Use an internal render helper (e.g., `renderContent()`, `renderBody()`) to encapsulate state branching
3. Early returns with different root types are acceptable for page-level components or when the component is intentionally designed to swap layouts (e.g., a full-page skeleton replacing the entire page shell)
